    "temperature": {
      "file": "air_receiver_dryer_package_temperature.glb",
      "title": "Chill to condense, re-heat to protect",
      "description": "Warm saturated air is pre-cooled by the outgoing cold air, then chilled in the evaporator to ≈ 3 °C so the water condenses and is drained. The cold dry air is re-heated in the air-to-air exchanger before leaving, which saves refrigeration energy and prevents pipe sweating. Pressure dew point ≈ +3 °C (ISO 8573-1 class 4) — fine for general plant air; instrument air needing −40 °C uses a desiccant dryer. The receiver buffers demand, settles condensate and smooths compressor cycling.",
      "render": "normal",
      "annotations": []
    }
  },
